ly guilty or not it  was all about the honor of the army  which apparently demanded that dreyfuss  be convicted regardless of the evidence  travis's second conviction sparked  international outcry france was accused  of being an arbitrary state contrary to  its supposed ideals even france's  closest ally russia ruled by one of  europe's last absolute monarchs thought  the verdict was wrongly decided there  was serious talk of boycotting the  world's fair to be held in paris the  next year prime minister waldek rousseau  had had enough he offered a compromise  instead of going through the ordeal of a  second retrial he offered dreyfus a  pardon that would free him immediately  unfortunately accepting a pardon also  requires a convicted person to accept  guilt for the crimes that they're being  pardoned for despite being initially  reluctant dreyfus ultimately accepted  leaving prison for the last time on  september 21 1899. it was a bitter pill  for the dre facades to swallow  especially when coupled with an amnesty  law passed in november that not only  cleared piccar and zola but also  prevented any of the conspirators that  had committed crimes to put dreifus in  prison from ever facing accountability  for it some of the more committed trey  facades who viewed the movement more as  one of social change than a campaign to  write the act of one injustice turned on  dreyfuss after he accepted the pardon  feeling like he had betrayed their cause  but it's hard to blame the man who after  all was innocent for not turning down a  chance to escape the hell he'd spent the  last five years in draper's hadn't  signed up for martyrdom all he wanted to  do was go home to his family most people  in his position would have probably done  the same thing  on july the 12th 1906 alfred dreyfus's  conviction at ren was officially quashed  by the supreme court this time there  would be no retrial dravis's 12-year  long legal ordeal was over by this time  the old leadership of the army had been  swept away replaced by a new generation  that wasn't so entrenched in their own  mystique to admit when a mistake had  been made organized anti-semitism had  largely fallen apart in france by 1902  and though it has never gone away it has  never again been embraced so openly by  the country not even when occupied by  the nazis during world war ii georges  pacar would eventually serve as minister  of war after being exonerated in the  amnesty of 1899. ferdinand esterhazy  would die in 1923 still in exile in  england under an assumed name alfred  dreyfuss was promoted to commandant the  day after his exoneration reflecting the  rank he would have had if his career  hadn't been disrupted he was also  decorated with the legion of honor the  only attempt at restitution that france  would ever give him the days of  multi-million euro settlements being  decades away not surprisingly drapers  didn't serve long in the army that had  betrayed him so viciously after being  rehabilitated retiring a year later he  returned to service during the first  world war seeing combat of adol among  other places he retired again in 1919 as  a colonel dreyfuss never really  recovered his health from his time spent  on devil's island his hair returned  permanently white and he was susceptible  to fevers and fatigue his sleep was  plagued by nightmares most likely caused  by untreated traumatic stress disorder  he was completely unable to separate  himself from the affair that had  consumed his life in 1908 while  attending the interment ceremony of emil  zola's remains in the pantheon in paris  anti-dre facade fnatic fired two shots  at him wounding him in the arm in the  last act of judicial bitterness the man  was acquitted after claiming he only  meant to wound dreyfus and wasn't trying  to kill him he died in 1935 quietly at  home surrounded by his family two days  later his funeral possession passed by  splendid formations of soldiers on its  way to his final resting place 